WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.020
Every SSH automation workflow uses an identity.

2
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:11.400
That identity may be represented by an SSH key, service account, deployment account, or job-specific credential.

3
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:19.660
Good identity design is what lets teams answer who or what performed a remote action, why it was allowed, and how access can be changed later.

4
00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:25.380
A common weak pattern is one shared privileged key used across many jobs and environments.

5
00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:34.520
That kind of key is difficult to attribute, difficult to scope, difficult to rotate, and difficult to revoke without breaking unrelated workflows.

6
00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:39.740
It also makes incidents harder to investigate because many scripts may appear to be the same actor.

7
00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.220
Dedicated automation identities are easier to manage.

8
00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:48.120
Each identity should have clear ownership, purpose, scope, and lifecycle.

9
00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:55.660
A deployment identity should not automatically be a diagnostic identity, a backup identity, and an administrative identity.

10
00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:00.720
Production access should be separated from development and staging access where practical.

11
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.720
Private keys must be protected.

12
00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:11.740
Storage location, file permissions, secret manager handling, passphrase strategy, and agent use all affect risk.

13
00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:19.120
Agents and passphrases can help some workflows, but they introduce operational tradeoffs that should be understood rather than copied blindly.

14
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:22.040
Authorized keys need ongoing review.

15
00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:28.620
Stale keys, abandoned accounts, unclear comments, and broad forced-command exceptions can accumulate over time.

16
00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:37.520
Secure automation includes inventory, ownership tracking, rotation, revocation, and cleanup of access that no longer has a business purpose.

17
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:41.800
The purpose of key lifecycle work is not paperwork.

18
00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:44.060
It keeps remote access understandable.

19
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:53.280
When a job changes, an owner leaves, a host is retired, or an environment boundary changes, the SSH identities behind automation should change with it.
